=== General pseudosuchian research ===
* Evidence interpreted as indicative of overall climatic niche conservatism of non-marine pseudosuchians throughout their evolutionary history is presented by Chiarenza et al. (2026), who also report evidence of persistence of habitats that were climatically suitable for these archosaurs throughout all but the most extreme scenarios of the [[Cretaceous–Paleogene extinction event]], providing a possibility for survival of crocodylomorphs in low-latitude refugia.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Chiarenza |first1=A. A. |last2=Howard |first2=A. F. C. |last3=Varnham |first3=G. L. |last4=Farnsworth |first4=A. |last5=Valdes |first5=P. J. |last6=Mannion |first6=P. D. |title=Climatic niche conservatism shapes the macroevolution, extinction, and survivorship of crocodile-line archosaurs |year=2026 |journal=Science Advances |volume=12 |issue=43 |article-number=eaeg2217 |doi=10.1126/sciadv.aeg2217 |doi-access=free }}</ref>
* Evidence from the study of the [[histology]] of appendicular elements of ''[[Saurosuchus|Saurosuchus galilei]]'', ''[[Sillosuchus|Sillosuchus longicervix]]'', ''[[Aetobarbakinoides|Aetobarbakinoides brasiliensis]]'', ''[[Aetosauroides|Aetosauroides scagliai]]'', ''[[Gracilisuchus|Gracilisuchus stipanicicorum]]'', ''[[Tarjadia|Tarjadia ruthae]]'' and ''[[Riojasuchus|Riojasuchus tenuisceps]]'', indicative of diversity of growth rates of the studied pseudosuchians, is presented by Ponce, Cerda & Desojo (2026).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Ponce |first1=D. A. |last2=Cerda |first2=I. A. |last3=Desojo |first3=J. B. |year=2026 |title=More diverse than expected: osteohistology of non-crocodylomorph Pseudosuchia from the Triassic of South America |journal=Palaeontology |volume=69 |issue=2 |article-number=e70056 |doi=10.1111/pala.70056 |bibcode=2026Palgy..6970056P }}</ref>

=== Aetosaur research ===
* Probable aetosaur dermal impressions are reported from the Upper Triassic New Haven Formation (Connecticut, United States) by Ben-Salahuddin, Hyatt & Brinkman (2026).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Ben-Salahuddin |first1=A. |last2=Hyatt |first2=J. A. |last3=Brinkman |first3=D. L. |year=2026 |title=Assessing Two New Sets of Impressions from the New Haven Formation (Late Triassic: Norian–Rhaetian) of New Haven, Connecticut |journal=Bulletin of the Peabody Museum of Natural History |volume=67 |issue=2 |pages=215–233 |doi=10.3374/014.067.0203 }}</ref>
* Haldar & Ray (2026) study the [[osteoderm]] histology of ''[[Venkatasuchus|Venkatasuchus armatum]]''.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Haldar |first1=A. |last2=Ray |first2=S. |year=2026 |title=New biological insights from osteoderm histology of ''Venkatasuchus armatum'' (Pseudosuchia: Aetosauria) |journal=The Anatomical Record |article-number=ar.70319 |doi=10.1002/ar.70319 |pmid=42618451 }}</ref>

=== Crocodylomorph research ===
* Revision of the fossil record of Early Jurassic to Pliocene crocodyliform eggs and nests is published by Panades, Galobart & Sellés (2026).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Panades |first1=X. |last2=Galobart |first2=À. |last3=Sellés |first3=A. |year=2026 |title=The fossil record of crocodyliform eggs, eggshells, and nests: a deep-time synthesis and evolutionary perspective |journal=Historical Biology: An International Journal of Paleobiology |pages=1–43 |doi=10.1080/08912963.2026.2710626 }}</ref>
* Paixão et al. (2026) describe isolated eggshells and egg clutches from the Upper Cretaceous [[Adamantina Formation]] (Brazil) with the egg arrangement similar to those seen in extant crocodilians, including an assemblage of at least 47 eggs representing the largest Mesozoic crocodyliform egg clutch reported to date.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Paixão |first1=G. M. X. |last2=Martinelli |first2=A. G. |last3=Marsola |first3=J. C. A. |last4=Hechenleitner |first4=E. M. |last5=Nava |first5=W. R. |last6=Chiappe |first6=L. M. |last7=Jussiani |first7=E. I. |last8=Rozadilla |first8=S. |last9=Kaluza |first9=J. |last10=Pinheiro |first10=F. L. |year=2026 |title=Fossil evidence of exceptionally large egg-clutches sheds light on reproductive diversity in Late Cretaceous crocodyliforms from Brazil |journal=Journal of Vertebrate Paleontology |volume=45 |issue=4 |article-number=e2596997 |doi=10.1080/02724634.2025.2596997 }}</ref>

