=== General non-avian dinosaur research ===
* Review of preceding studies and of methods used to study dinosaur biology and evolution is published by [[Xu Xing (paleontologist)|Xu]] et al. (2026).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Xu |first1=X. |last2=Upchurch |first2=P. |last3=Zanno |first3=L. |last4=McNamara |first4=M. |last5=Qin |first5=Z. |last6=Benton |first6=M. |title=Progress and future directions in dinosaur palaeontology |year=2026 |journal=Nature Reviews Biodiversity |volume=2 |issue=9 |pages=546–564 |doi=10.1038/s44358-026-00191-9 |url=https://research-information.bris.ac.uk/en/publications/b6e0263d-38f4-4bf6-80cd-18100a80a6e5 }}</ref>
* Henderson (2026) determines rates of discovery of new dinosaur taxa and changes geographical origin of new discoveries throughout the history of the study of the group.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Henderson |first=D. M. |year=2026 |title=200 years of dinosaur discoveries –a visual portrayal of their geographic and historical origins |journal=PeerJ |volume=14 |article-number=e21351 |doi=10.7717/peerj.21351 |doi-access=free |pmid=42222490 |pmc=13221989 |bibcode=2026PeerJ..1421351H }}</ref>

=== Saurischian research ===
* Müller (2026) describes new [[Herrerasauria|herrerasaur]] fossil material from the Upper Triassic strata of the [[Santa Maria Formation]] (Brazil), providing evidence of persistence of members of this group in the studied area after the transition from [[Rhynchosauria|rhynchosaur]]-dominated assemblages to ones dominated by [[Traversodontidae|traversodontid]] [[Cynodontia|cynodonts]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Müller |first=R. T. |year=2026 |title=First occurrence of a herrerasaurid dinosaur in a traversodontid cynodont-dominated Late Triassic fauna of southern Brazil |journal=The Science of Nature |volume=113 |issue=6 |article-number=133 |doi=10.1007/s00114-026-02185-1 |doi-access=free }}</ref>
* Pawlak et al. (2026) identify [[lungfish]] [[aestivation]] burrows in the Triassic strata of the [[Ørsted Dal Formation]] (Greenland), interpreted as indicative of a seasonally dry climate in the studied area during the late [[Norian]], and indicating that aridity was not a barrier for dispersal of theropods and sauropodomorphs living in the studied area at the time.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Pawlak |first1=W. |last2=Tałanda |first2=M. |last3=Sulej |first3=T. |last4=Qvarnström |first4=M. |last5=Niedźwiedzki |first5=G. |year=2026 |title=High-latitude dipnoan aestivation burrows suggest seasonal aridity for early dinosaur ecosystems in the Late Triassic of East Greenland |journal=Palaeogeography, Palaeoclimatology, Palaeoecology |volume=689 |article-number=113624 |doi=10.1016/j.palaeo.2026.113624 |bibcode=2026PPP...68913624P }}</ref>

==== Theropod research ====
* Boisvert et al. (2026) compare the median size classes of ceratosaurians, megalosauroids, allosauroids, megaraptorans, and tyrannosauroids throughout the Jurassic and Cretaceous periods, and report evidence of a higher median number of missing size classes in tyrannosauroid-dominated ecosystems compared to allosauroid/megalosauroid-dominated ones.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Boisvert |first1=C. |last2=Perkins |first2=J. |last3=Morrison |first3=C. |last4=Gascoigne |first4=S. J. L. |last5=Holtz |first5=T. R. |last6=Curtice |first6=B. |year=2026 |title=Obligate faunivorous megatheropod size class patterns across the Jurassic-Cretaceous Periods |journal=PeerJ |volume=14 |article-number=e21007 |doi=10.7717/peerj.21007 |doi-access=free |pmid=41978889 |pmc=13070321 |bibcode=2026PeerJ..1421007B }}</ref>
* A study on the skull biomechanics and likely feeding behaviors of members of diverse theropod subgroups, with a focus on [[Tyrannosauroidea|tyrannosauroids]], is published by Johnson-Ransom et al. (2026).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Johnson-Ransom |first1=E. |last2=Gignac |first2=P. |last3=Barta |first3=D. E. |last4=Felice |first4=R. N. |last5=Snively |first5=E. |year=2026 |title=Comparative cranial biomechanics reveal macroevolutionary trends in theropod dinosaurs, with emphasis on Tyrannosauroidea |journal=The Anatomical Record |volume=309 |issue=11 |article-number=ar.70126 |doi=10.1002/ar.70126 |pmid=41589510 |doi-access=free |pmc=13644910 }}</ref>

==== Sauropodomorph research ====
* Schuurman, Bruner & Knoll (2026) reconstruct the topology of skulls of 14 sauropodomorph species spanning the entire evolutionary history of the group, and report probable evidence of limitation of morphological changes of individual bones by spatial constraints.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Schuurman |first1=T. |last2=Bruner |first2=E. |last3=Knoll |first3=F. |title=Anatomical network analysis of sauropodomorph cranial topology |year=2026 |journal=Palaeontology |volume=69 |issue=4 |article-number=e70079 |doi=10.1111/pala.70079 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2026Palgy..6970079S }}</ref>
* A study on the evolution of the humeral morphology of sauropodomorphs, providing evidence of different pattern of evolution of sauropod-like traits in the proximal and distal half of the humerus, is published by Lefebvre et al. (2026).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Lefebvre |first1=R. |last2=Partout |first2=L. |last3=Mallison |first3=H. |last4=Houssaye |first4=A. |title=Partial analyses of humeral shape in sauropodomorph dinosaurs highlight a hidden modularity and the differential evolution of sauropod bauplan-related traits |year=2026 |journal=Palaeontology |volume=69 |issue=2 |article-number=e70048 |doi=10.1111/pala.70048 |bibcode=2026Palgy..6970048L }}</ref>
* Foster et al. (2026) review the history of excavation and study of the fossil material of ''[[Dystrophaeus|Dystrophaeus viaemalae]]'', review the geological setting of the fossil material, and interpret the morphology of the fossil material of ''D. viaemalae'' (including additional material collected since 2014) as unlikely to be a member of Diplodocoidea.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Foster |first1=J. R. |last2=Trujillo |first2=K. C. |last3=Irmis |first3=R. B. |last4=Wedel |first4=M. J. |last5=Chamberlain |first5=K. R. |last6=Gillette |first6=D. D. |last7=McMullen |first7=S. K. |title=History, age, and stratigraphy of the type locality of ''Dystrophaeus viaemalae'' (Sauropoda) in the lower Morrison Formation of southeastern Utah |year=2026 |journal=New Mexico Museum of Natural History and Science Bulletin |volume=102 |pages=201–213 |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/399997614 }}</ref>

=== Ornithischian research ===
* Reid, Fonseca & Madzia (2026) evaluate the history of ornithischian clade names and their synonymy.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Reid |first1=I. J. |last2=Fonseca |first2=A. O. |last3=Madzia |first3=D. |year=2026 |title=The evolution of ornithischian nomenclature |journal=Zoological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=207 |issue=3 |article-number=zlag111 |doi=10.1093/zoolinnean/zlag111 }}</ref>
* A study on the life history of ''[[Changchunsaurus|Changchunsaurus parvus]]'', providing evidence of one of the slowest growth rates reported in dinosaurs, is published by Wang et al. (2026).<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Wang |first1=L. |last2=Maho |first2=T. |last3=Chen |first3=J. |last4=Young |first4=J. M. |last5=Reisz |first5=R. R. |year=2026 |title=Exceptionally slow growth rates and life history of ''Changchunsaurus parvus'' from the Upper Cretaceous Quantou Formation, Songliao Basin, Northeast China |journal=Palaeogeography, Palaeoclimatology, Palaeoecology |volume=698 |article-number=113931 |doi=10.1016/j.palaeo.2026.113931 |bibcode=2026PPP...69813931W }}</ref>

==== Thyreophoran research ====
* Sánchez-Fenollosa, Cobos & Suñer (2026) describe new stegosaurian fossil material from the [[Villar del Arzobispo Formation]] (Spain), and revise the fossil record of Stegosauria from the eastern part of the Iberian Peninsula.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Sánchez-Fenollosa |first1=S. |last2=Cobos |first2=A. |last3=Suñer |first3=M. |year=2026 |title=The plated dinosaurs (Ornithischia, Stegosauria) of eastern Iberia (Spain): Taxonomy, diversity, and ecology |journal=Palaeontologia Electronica |volume=29 |issue=1 |article-number=29.1.a13 |doi=10.26879/1637 |doi-access=free }}</ref>
* [[ReBecca Hunt-Foster|Hunt-Foster]] et al. (2026) describe portions of forelimbs of an indeterminate stegosaurid from the Brushy Basin Member of the Morrison Formation (Utah, United States), estimated to be the largest stegosaurid specimen from the Morrison Formation reported to date.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Hunt-Foster |first1=R. K. |last2=Woodruff |first2=D. C. |last3=Sroka |first3=S. D. |last4=Foster |first4=J. R. |title=The largest reported stegosaurid from the Morrison Formation (Upper Jurassic) |year=2026 |journal=New Mexico Museum of Natural History and Science Bulletin |volume=102 |pages=287–292 |url=https://www.researchgate.net/publication/400050523 }}</ref>

==== Cerapod research ====
* Kim, Lee & Gihm (2026) report the discovery of a trackway of a running ornithopod from the Lower Cretaceous (Aptian–Albian) strata of the [[Hasandong Formation]] (South Korea).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Kim |first1=S. |last2=Lee |first2=Y.-N. |last3=Gihm |first3=Y. S. |year=2026 |title=A running ornithopod trackway from the Hasandong Formation (Lower Cretaceous, Korea): Evidence of a 'Goldilocks' substrate window and rapid avulsion burial |journal=Palaeoworld |volume=35 |issue=6 |article-number=201155 |doi=10.1016/j.palwor.2026.201155 |bibcode=2026Palae..3501155K }}</ref>
* Rotatori et al. (2026) study the phylogenetic relationships of [[iguanodontia]]n ornithopods and reconstruct the evolutionary history of the group, name a new clade [[Ouranosauria]], and trace the major radiation of Iguanodontia to the Early Jurassic (Pliensbachian–Toarcian).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Rotatori |first1=F. M. |last2=Chiarenza |first2=A. A. |last3=Fanti |first3=F. |last4=Moreno-Azanza |first4=M. |year=2026 |title=The early origin of Iguanodontia: new insights into the macroevolution, diversity and biogeography of the clade |journal=Palaeontology |volume=69 |issue=2 |article-number=e70057 |doi=10.1111/pala.70057 |bibcode=2026Palgy..6970057R }}</ref>

