=== Avian research ===
* Evidence from the study of birds and extinct bird-line archosaurs, linking changes of shape of [[semicircular canals]] at the time of the dinosaur–bird transition and the origin of avian flight to morphological changes in the head-neck system of birds and their relatives rather than to sensory function, is presented by Navalón et al. (2026).<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Navalón |first1=G. |last2=Evers |first2=S. W. |last3=Hughes |first3=E. |last4=Bjarnason |first4=A. |last5=Chiappe |first5=L. M. |last6=Walsh |first6=S. A. |last7=Neenan |first7=J. M. |last8=Panciroli |first8=E. |last9=Field |first9=D. J. |last10=Benson |first10=R. B. J. |year=2026 |title=Coordinated transformations of the labyrinth and occiput during the dinosaur–bird transition |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America |volume=123 |issue=42 |article-number=e2612503123 |doi=10.1073/pnas.2612503123 |pmid=42863076 }}</ref>
* Benito et al. (2026) contest the conclusions of the study of Wilken et al. (2025)<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wilken |first1=A. T. |last2=Sellers |first2=K. C. |last3=Cost |first3=I. N. |last4=David |first4=J. |last5=Middleton |first5=K. M. |last6=Witmer |first6=L. M. |last7=Holliday |first7=C. M. |year=2025 |title=Avian cranial kinesis is the result of increased encephalization during the origin of birds |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America |volume=122 |issue=13 |at=e2411138122 |doi=10.1073/pnas.2411138122 |doi-access=free |pmid=40096621 |pmc=12002250 |bibcode=2025PNAS..12211138W }}</ref> about the evolution of the ability of birds to move parts of the skull independently, arguing that these conclusions were based on inadequate taxon sampling and morphological misinterpretations;<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Benito |first1=J. |last2=Kuo |first2=P.-C. |last3=Torres |first3=C. R. |last4=Navalón |first4=G. |last5=Plateau |first5=O. |last6=Clark |first6=A. D. |last7=Steell |first7=E. M. |last8=Field |first8=D. J. |year=2026 |title=Shouldering the challenge of deciphering avian palate evolution |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America |volume=123 |issue=1 |article-number=e2514111123 |doi=10.1073/pnas.2514111123 |pmid=41481437 |pmc=12773698 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2026PNAS..12314111B }}</ref> in response Wilken et al. (2026) agree that the bone interpreted in the 2025 study as a coracoid of ''[[Janavis]]'' is more likely to be a pterygoid, but question the affinities of this bone among Mesozoic birds, and overall reaffirm their original conclusion that powered prokinesis is most likely an [[autapomorphy]] of [[Neognathae|neognath]] birds.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Wilken |first1=A. T. |last2=Sellers |first2=K. C. |last3=David |first3=J. L. |last4=Witmer |first4=L. M. |last5=Holliday |first5=C. M. |year=2026 |title=Reply to Benito et al.: Problems in the Cretaceous evolution of the avian palatobasal joint |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the United States of America |volume=123 |issue=1 |article-number=e2520865123 |doi=10.1073/pnas.2520865123 |pmid=41481457 |pmc=12773701 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2026PNAS..12320865W }}</ref>

